{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And Israel sent messengers to Sihon… \"Let me pass through your land\"</b> – Even though they were not commanded to send them terms of peace, they opened with peace, in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 21:22). And I will further explain, with the help of HaShem, in its place (Deuteronomy 20:10), that concerning all the nations they were commanded to open with terms of peace, except for Ammon and Moab. But in truth, what he said to him, <b>\"Let me pass through your land,\"</b> this was Moses doing on his own initiative by way of conciliation, for the land of Sihon and Og was the inheritance of Israel, for it belongs to the Amorite. And by strict justice, if they had answered peace and opened to them, the entire people found in it should have become tributary and served them. But Moses knew that Israel at this time would not conquer all ten nations, and he desired that all their conquest be across the Jordan and beyond, so that their settlement would be together, and so that it would be the good land, which flows with milk and honey. Do you not see that if the children of Gad and the children of Reuben had not requested it from him, he would not have left a single person there, but would have left it desolate? And so it is stated in the Sifre (Tavo 309): \"To give you\" — this excludes Transjordan, which you took on your own. And our Rabbis further said (Numbers Rabbah 7:8), in the passage of the ten sanctities, that Transjordan is not fit for the Temple and for the dwelling of the Shekhinah. And so it appears from Scripture, which says: \"But if the land of your possession is unclean\" etc. (Joshua 22:19). And they did not send words of peace to Og, for he, as soon as he saw that they had struck Sihon, went out to meet them for battle."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:31.029652050Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}